WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.647
A SOC 2 Type I report evaluates control design and suitability at a
specific point in time.

2
00:00:06.647 --> 00:00:13.685
The central question is whether the organization has described the system
and controls appropriately and whether those controls

3
00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:19.550
are suitably designed to meet the applicable Trust Services Criteria as of
the report date.

4
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.287
It is a snapshot, not a movie.

5
00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:26.588
Type I can be useful early in a SOC 2 program.

6
00:00:26.588 --> 00:00:33.235
A first-time organization may need to show customers that it has defined
the system, selected appropriate criteria,

7
00:00:33.235 --> 00:00:37.536
mapped controls, assigned owners, and put key control designs in place.

8
00:00:37.536 --> 00:00:44.574
A Type I report can help validate that the control environment is ready to
operate and can support

9
00:00:44.574 --> 00:00:51.221
customer assurance while the organization prepares for a later Type II
review period. The focus is design.

10
00:00:51.221 --> 00:00:58.258
For example, an access review control may state that user access to in-
scope systems is reviewed quarterly by

11
00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:00.058
responsible managers.

12
00:01:00.058 --> 00:01:06.705
A Type I audit may look at whether that control is defined, assigned,
aligned to the criteria,

13
00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:10.224
and supported by a process that could reasonably work.

14
00:01:10.224 --> 00:01:14.916
It may inspect evidence that the control exists at the report date,

15
00:01:14.916 --> 00:01:20.781
but it does not show that quarterly reviews happened consistently for
months. That limitation matters.

16
00:01:20.781 --> 00:01:26.255
A Type I report does not prove that controls operated over a long period.

17
00:01:26.255 --> 00:01:32.902
It does not demonstrate that change management was followed repeatedly,
that monitoring alerts were reviewed over time,

18
00:01:32.902 --> 00:01:37.594
that vendors were reviewed on schedule, or that exceptions were
consistently remediated.

19
00:01:37.594 --> 00:01:40.722
It can show readiness, but not sustained operation.

20
00:01:40.722 --> 00:01:44.241
Organizations sometimes overuse Type I language in sales conversations.

21
00:01:44.241 --> 00:01:46.587
It is better to be precise.

22
00:01:46.587 --> 00:01:50.106
Type I can provide point-in-time assurance about control design.

23
00:01:50.106 --> 00:01:55.580
It may be appropriate when a program is new, when customers want early
evidence,

24
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.881
or when the organization needs a structured milestone before Type II.

25
00:01:59.881 --> 00:02:06.919
But customers with mature vendor risk programs may eventually expect Type
II because they want assurance that controls

26
00:02:06.919 --> 00:02:09.656
operate, not just that they are designed.

27
00:02:09.656 --> 00:02:13.957
For teams preparing for Type I, the work is still real.

28
00:02:13.957 --> 00:02:17.476
The system description must be accurate. Controls need owners.

29
00:02:17.476 --> 00:02:19.822
Policies need to match actual processes.

30
00:02:19.822 --> 00:02:23.732
Evidence should be honest and traceable. Exceptions should be documented.

31
00:02:23.732 --> 00:02:30.534
Type I is not the finish line; it is often the first disciplined
checkpoint on the path to

32
00:02:30.534 --> 00:02:30.534
sustained readiness.
